    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a textbook instance of the codebase's established endpoint-validation pattern, and every piece lands in the right unit:\n\n- **`ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate`** is a direct sibling of `ValidateOwnerPostcode`: one validation concern, `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto)`, optional-when-absent skip, throws a checked exception. This is the right home \u2014 validation guards are their own function classes in this project, not helper methods.\n- **Not folded into `RegistrationDates`** \u2014 correctly so. `RegistrationDates` is a date-*derivation* helper (effective/business-day/count); a 400-throwing validation guard is a distinct concern and belongs in a step, so keeping it separate avoids turning that helper into a catch-all.\n- **Not folded into `ValidateOwner`** \u2014 also correct. `ValidateOwner` does bean-validation binding + republishing (`Out`); a field-level business rule is a separate step, matching how `ValidateOwnerPostcode` sits apart. Wiring it right after `validate` and before the normalize steps is consistent placement.\n- **Handler and exception** are byte-for-byte parallel to `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` / `InvalidPostcodeException` (checked exception, single-`String` ctor, `ProblemDetails.build` at 400, escalation YAML keyed on the FQCN). Nothing duplicated, nothing novel introduced where a convention already existed.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this entirely natural.",
